WEBVTT

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.100
brought to you in living color on NBC.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:40.880
Scott, I can't thank you enough for this training session.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.920
I've learned more from you in the last three weeks than an entire semester at the Academy.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.620
It's my pleasure, Lassie.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.190
Great to be a miracle worker again.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.430
Thanks to Jory.

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:55.190
Mandela Force, I've got my space legs back again.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.980
Forgive me for asking, what...

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.860
What was it like being stuck in a transporter for 75 years?

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.220
Disorienting, especially when you realize all your friends are dead and gone.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.500
Oh, sorry. I didn't mean to make you upset.

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:19.400
Never mind, Les. At my age, it's too easy to be Marus. Let's talk about happier things.

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:24.580
We're less than a parsec from the Lapa system. Have you ever been here before?

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:29.960
Aye, Les, we did. We're sort of frontier back.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:56.160
back then uninhabited or so we thought take it easy Rob this is the fourth failure this month

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.880
and to top it off we have Enterprise and our chief engineer watching over

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.410
shoulder no offense okay this is cutting edge technology remember have a little patience i told

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:11.570
the science council i could terraform lap of three in less than a decade at this rate it will take

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:16.470
twice that long and you know how well the science council does with patients that's still a whole

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:22.640
century faster than anybody else has done it before doctor listen to the man will you if

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:29.220
this works no when this works we'll be able to terraform worlds for millions of victims of war

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:29.680
payment

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.810
disaster. I suppose you're both right.

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:35.390
I just hate to break a promise. Look,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.620
why don't you take the inspection of Section 12

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:40.350
yourself? If the pylon is failing,

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.420
you'll be there for the fix. That,

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:45.900
Dr. DeWitt, very good idea.

00:02:46.730 --> 00:02:48.910
Doctor, would you mind if I tagged along?

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.820
You're using transporter technology in a whole new way.

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.340
I'd like to see what you're doing. Might be a good idea

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:55.920
to have another pair of eyes on the problem.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.520
And hands. Great.

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:37.690
Autographs left outpost were on course for section 12 and should have inspection completed in approximately 11.6 minutes.

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:20.030
controls inoperative.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:37.510
Space.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.030
The final frontier.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.000
These are the voyages of the Starship Enterprise.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.270
Its five-year mission?

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.760
To explore strange new worlds.

00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.540
To seek out new life and new civilization.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.410
to boldly go where no man has gone before.

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:43.840
in orbit of Lappa III on the edge of the frontier.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:48.900
We've recovered Chief Engineer Scott and one other research team member from a crashed shuttlecraft

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:53.920
after a massive explosion ripped apart thousands of kilometers of the planet's surface.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.990
We have no idea what caused the death of those dozen brave scientists.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:05:59.980
For now, we must tend to...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:09.290
are wounded doctor would you quit poking at me i feel fine scotty sit back and relax your skull's

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:16.520
not half as thick as we think it is dr marcus oh she's fine she'll be out of here before you are

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:23.620
a couple of bruises mild concussion some cracked ribs she must be made into tronium scotty what

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:29.060
happened no idea captain there's nothing at the outpost that should have went up that way

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:32.670
Why can I speak with Dr. Marcus?

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:38.090
She'd be conscious any time now, but she's in no shape for an interrogation.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.910
Bones, we need to know what happened to that outpost.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.040
Considering we're on the edge of the frontier, that's why I need to talk with her.

00:06:45.480 --> 00:06:46.460
Talk with me about what?

00:06:47.530 --> 00:06:50.240
Whoa, easy. Easy there. How are you feeling?

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:52.510
I'm fine. Where am I?

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:54.400
On board the Starship Enterprise.

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:58.150
I'm Captain James T. Kirk, and Chief Medical Officer, Larry McCoy.

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:02.820
What happened to the outpost? Why am I here?

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.760
The Lappa III outpost was destroyed.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:11.320
Destroyed?

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:13.220
There was an explosion.

00:07:14.210 --> 00:07:18.210
A huge chunk of the planet's surface along with the outpost was incinerated.

00:07:19.550 --> 00:07:23.720
Except for you and my chief engineer, they're no survivors.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:36.900
Dr. Mars, can I ask you a few questions?

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:38.170
No.

00:07:39.560 --> 00:07:40.900
Okay, we need to do this.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:52.170
We need to inspect a terraforming pylon.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:55.040
Is something...

00:07:57.240 --> 00:07:59.960
You crashed about 200 kilometers from the outside.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.740
post on the backside of a mountain ridge otherwise you wouldn't be here mr scott sent a distress

00:08:06.740 --> 00:08:13.940
call and we beamed you aboard you don't know why the planet exploded i think you might be able to

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:14.440
offer some clues

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:17.720
Have you had any alien contact?

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:22.260
No. No, we haven't had any alien activity out here.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:25.540
The shuttlecraft sensors are clear. That much I remember.

00:08:25.880 --> 00:08:28.800
We'll start the investigation as an accident.

00:08:31.532 --> 00:08:32.372
Captain.

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.280
I have to be part of the investigation.

00:08:37.036 --> 00:08:37.776
I need to be.

00:08:37.996 --> 00:08:38.996
With all due respect-

00:08:38.996 --> 00:08:40.396
Those people were my colleagues.

00:08:41.452 --> 00:08:42.092
My friends.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:44.740
I could have died with them.

00:08:45.164 --> 00:08:45.784
And I didn't.

00:08:46.924 --> 00:08:48.364
They're my responsibility.

00:08:48.876 --> 00:08:49.596
I owe them.

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:50.680
Captain.

00:08:51.084 --> 00:08:52.084
I owe them an answer.

00:08:55.564 --> 00:08:57.064
Dr. McCoy believes you're up to it?

00:08:57.324 --> 00:08:58.904
At least somebody cares about my opinion.

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:04.680
Listen, if you start to feel tired or confused, dazed in any way, I want to see you immediately.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:08.650
I want to see you anyway and every 12 hours until I clear you.

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.150
I'll be working with my first officer, Commander Spock.

00:09:13.260 --> 00:09:13.820
Captain Kirk.

00:09:16.400 --> 00:09:16.700
Thank you.

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:22.030
I tried to think what I'd do in this situation.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:24.560
I had to disobey the authority.

00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:28.400
I assumed you were going to do the same, Dr. Marcus.

00:09:28.620 --> 00:09:28.940
Please.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:30.920
Call me Carol.

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:32.850
Jim.

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:44.930
Jim.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:48.610
You need to realize, Dr. Marcus is dealing with a terrible tragedy.

00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:50.530
She is still in shock.

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:55.360
I'm not a doctor.

00:09:56.040 --> 00:09:58.940
No one knows the technology on LAPA 3 better than Carol Marcus.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:09:59.960
We need-

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:01.960
And she needs to be handled with care.

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:07.260
She needs someone who is sensitive enough to understand her emotional needs at this time.

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:09.950
And you gave her Spock.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:13.830
Okay, I'll do it.

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:14.150
You.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:16.480
Why so surprised?

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:18.860
I'm not.

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:24.160
Mr. Spock?

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.090
Captain, I'm working on my analysis of the explosion.

00:10:27.410 --> 00:10:29.400
I need to interview Dr. Marcus.

00:10:30.028 --> 00:10:30.908
What do you have so far?

00:10:31.340 --> 00:10:35.740
The terraforming project was designed to use modified transporter technology.

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:42.880
Restructuring the atmosphere while assembling organic molecules from raw materials would require considerable energy.

00:10:43.564 --> 00:10:47.324
but none of the matter-antimatter reactors available to the project

00:10:47.468 --> 00:10:52.668
would be sufficient enough to vaporize 100 million square kilometers of the planet's surface.

00:10:53.932 --> 00:10:56.172
As soon as you're clear, Mr. Scott, have him report to the bridge.

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:15.670
Subspace channel. Whisper. Mr. Scott. Quiet to a moment ago, sir. Did we pick something up on

00:11:15.670 --> 00:11:26.140
long-range sensors? A ship? Or a sensor ghost? Subspace chatter. It's gone now. Hmm. Begin a

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:29.960
standard mapping survey of the region. There is a ship out there that I think we're just getting

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.000
to know the territory.

00:11:32.364 --> 00:11:33.084
Maintain course.

00:11:34.508 --> 00:11:36.108
Keep on as if we ever noticed anything.

00:11:41.932 --> 00:11:42.292
to office.

00:11:43.084 --> 00:11:45.084
This is Mr. Spock, our science officer.

00:11:47.564 --> 00:11:48.384
You honor me, doctor.

00:11:49.324 --> 00:11:50.744
Your reputation precedes you.

00:11:50.964 --> 00:11:52.484
You honor me, Mr. Spock.

00:11:53.196 --> 00:11:56.496
May I ask you a few questions about your work on Lapa 3?

00:11:57.036 --> 00:11:57.516
Of course.

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:04.450
You used a series of matter-antimatter reactors for power, correct?

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:07.990
A series of modified reactors. We used massive energy.

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:12.380
Indeed. Did you modify or enhance any other component?

00:12:12.770 --> 00:12:15.530
The molecular converters, of course. That's the heart of the project.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:20.380
May I ask permission to run a background check on your entire project team?

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:22.520
What do you mean by that?

00:12:23.410 --> 00:12:27.010
I need to explore all possibilities, including sabotage.

00:12:27.210 --> 00:12:29.680
I know these people. Knew.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:33.280
Those people. I trusted them with my life.

00:12:33.460 --> 00:12:35.520
Yes, and you almost died.

00:12:36.630 --> 00:12:40.290
Perhaps there is a possibility that your trust may have been placed.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:44.350
Well, that's one possibility I won't consider.

00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:47.870
Is there somewhere I can go to review the outpost data?

00:12:48.150 --> 00:12:50.590
There's an ensign in the hall. He'll escort you to your quarters.

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:51.490
Thank you.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:57.770
Think you could have handled that with just a little warm tact?

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:04.820
She's the only survivor of a disaster that killed 26 of her closest friends.

00:13:05.630 --> 00:13:08.150
And you just accused one of them of being a suicide bomber.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:08.690
Accused?

00:13:10.150 --> 00:13:13.470
The purpose of the background check would be to clear her people from suspicion.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:15.270
I made no accusation.

00:13:15.530 --> 00:13:15.730
Yeah.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:20.340
Well, I'm making the accusation that Vulcans have absolutely no bedside manner.

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:50.330
with Dr. Mark is not very helpful.

00:13:52.640 --> 00:13:55.580
Dealing with a lot, you have to allow for that.

00:13:55.990 --> 00:13:59.930
Yes, motion is often the reason for being a lot.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:05.800
Permission to beam down to the planet's surface and explore the blast site.

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:07.400
Is it safe?

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:09.760
Radiation levels are high.

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:13.600
The Class K atmosphere will require us to wear environmental suits.

00:14:14.860 --> 00:14:15.940
They should shield us from her.

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:19.170
Dr. Marcus will want to go too.

00:14:20.300 --> 00:14:24.080
Captain, do not require her help.

00:14:24.950 --> 00:14:29.240
If she's still having emotional outbursts, she will only be a hindrance.

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:32.280
Emotional outbursts.

00:14:33.110 --> 00:14:39.040
If I understand correctly, she believes I accused one of her people of sabotaging the project.

00:14:40.450 --> 00:14:43.780
She believes you accused one of her people?

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:45.560
Yes.

00:14:48.950 --> 00:14:54.140
Sickbay, is there a sensitivity training for Vulcans?

00:14:58.800 --> 00:14:59.280
Thank you, Doc.

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:07.500
Lieutenant, the moment you get a fix on that blip, buzz me immediately.

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:08.240
Aye, sir.

00:15:08.860 --> 00:15:10.100
Scotty, you have the car.

00:16:04.570 --> 00:16:05.410
Hope I'm not intruding.

00:16:07.010 --> 00:16:07.870
Not at all, Captain.

00:16:09.310 --> 00:16:20.400
Jim, I apologize for my first officer.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:22.140
He means well.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:23.450
No apology necessary.

00:16:24.050 --> 00:16:25.050
He asked the right question.

00:16:25.330 --> 00:16:26.650
I just didn't like it.

00:16:30.860 --> 00:16:32.100
I see you're studying Spock's report.

00:16:33.300 --> 00:16:34.460
He's very thorough.

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:35.900
He's a Vulcan.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:37.020
Yeah, I noticed.

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:39.810
There isn't much to go on though.

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:42.600
Only the sensor data from the shuttlecraft.

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:46.530
The outpost just exploded.

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:49.190
No reason.

00:16:56.970 --> 00:16:58.090
Beamed down to the planet with him.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:16:59.170
He knows.

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:01.560
You gonna be all right?

00:17:06.160 --> 00:17:09.370
Yes. Yes, I will.

00:17:14.060 --> 00:17:17.600
Mr. Spock, will he be okay with my presence?

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:18.940
My emotion?

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:21.980
It's a deal with Dr. McCoy almost every day.

00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:23.910
Oh, of course.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:29.980
When I was a little girl, I...

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:39.650
collected creatures. All kinds. Insects and lizards and mice and birds. I was totally obsessed.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:46.860
My mother got me a micro-holographic imager, the kind they use in the hospitals. I could scan and

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:55.560
study the animals, the way they lived, breathed, reproduced, their whole life cycles. What a hobby.

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:59.080
My mom. She believed women should follow their dreams.

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.790
that would led you to your career in terraforming?

00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:07.020
No, it was my post-graduate work on the famine.

00:18:07.320 --> 00:18:11.360
There were too many people to evacuate,

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.180
and the Federation couldn't bring enough food in to feed them all.

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:15.940
It was heartbreaking.

00:18:17.180 --> 00:18:19.820
There was a planet that could support life in the system.

00:18:20.140 --> 00:18:24.880
They'd already started terraforming, but it would take 100 years.

00:18:27.200 --> 00:18:29.480
So I started thinking of ways to do it faster.

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:32.840
I guess that's the mother in me.

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:36.540
Oh, I, uh, didn't realize you had children.

00:18:37.510 --> 00:18:38.900
No, I don't.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:41.200
I'm birthing new worlds.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:43.580
New places for life to arise.

00:18:44.740 --> 00:18:47.280
That's the most mothering act of all, wouldn't you say?

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:49.150
Mother is a mother still.

00:18:50.050 --> 00:18:51.390
The holiest thing alive.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:53.570
Samuel Taylor Coleridge.

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:55.370
You read the classics.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:18:59.970
Only there's nothing holiest.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:01.700
about what happened on Lapa 3.

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:06.740
Carol, listen to me.

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:10.790
People like us, you and I,

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:12.730
we're entrusted with authority.

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:14.940
We have to make decisions.

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:16.440
Difficult decisions.

00:19:17.820 --> 00:19:19.560
Sometimes those decisions have risks.

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:24.030
Sometimes, sometimes people die.

00:19:24.980 --> 00:19:25.600
Part of the job.

00:19:26.250 --> 00:19:27.230
And to care for them,

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:28.540
what happens to them

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:29.830
is part of the job too.

00:19:32.110 --> 00:19:34.190
We don't have to like it, but we have to accept it.

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:38.380
Otherwise, we just paralyze ourselves.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:45.480
We have to be worthy of the trust others put in us because we have to.

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:52.660
Excuse me.

00:19:53.370 --> 00:19:56.420
I have to get to the bridge.

00:20:03.730 --> 00:20:26.900
Lost in the background, radiation like this.

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:28.730
No, this isn't from our equipment.

00:20:29.370 --> 00:20:29.970
Signature is complete.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:39.610
completely unfamiliar. This seems like a logical place to start. Indeed. These coordinates. Doctor.

00:21:11.130 --> 00:21:14.200
this station. Bring it online.

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:27.920
It's COVID.

00:21:30.000 --> 00:21:31.740
and its inhabitants almost instantly.

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:37.890
Perhaps there is comfort in knowing that your colleagues died immediately without pain.

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.240
Mr. Spock, it's a very nice try.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:47.360
Kirk to a landing party, come in.

00:21:52.620 --> 00:21:54.530
I wouldn't say.

00:21:54.710 --> 00:21:56.790
You make of it, Carol?

00:21:57.750 --> 00:21:58.790
The inside of a crater.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:05.140
detailed report when I get back to the Enterprise, Captain. Right now, I have work to do.

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:13.460
Very well, Doctor. Signal me when you're ready to beam up. Kirk out.

00:22:34.410 --> 00:22:35.330
You busy?

00:22:36.100 --> 00:22:37.720
Whatever gave you that idea?

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:40.880
Ever since Chappell left for medical exams,

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:42.920
I've been as tranquil as a Tribble

00:22:42.920 --> 00:22:45.120
in a Klingon stampede.

00:22:46.240 --> 00:22:46.900
What's on your mind, Jim?

00:22:48.410 --> 00:22:50.070
Carol. Dr. Marcus.

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:52.090
What about it?

00:22:52.900 --> 00:22:54.600
It's awkward.

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:57.400
I mean, she's holding up, but she isn't...

00:22:59.340 --> 00:22:59.980
I don't know how...

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:05.860
So I take it my role as ship's counselor is secure.

00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:08.160
I'll stick with command. Thanks.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:09.270
It's safer.

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:29.980
It makes you prone to negative responses even when there's a truck.

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.700
You make it sound like a stomachache.

00:23:33.340 --> 00:23:34.780
Touch of emotional turmoil.

00:23:35.740 --> 00:23:37.860
It is a condition common to humans.

00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:40.670
What?

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:45.460
This isn't the first time you've had to deal with an attractive woman.

00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:46.790
Bones.

00:23:48.050 --> 00:23:49.870
No romantic interest in her.

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:51.700
That would be wrong.

00:23:52.180 --> 00:23:53.020
And improper.

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:53.780
As a cat.

00:23:54.560 --> 00:23:56.860
Oh, I agree, but that's not what I meant.

00:23:58.430 --> 00:23:59.350
Bones don't feel pain.

00:24:00.700 --> 00:24:21.060
That would be inaccurate. The disciplines involve management of the physical processes experienced as emotion, resulting in what you perceive to be a kind of stoicism, several ways for a human to achieve even a level of adequacy.

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:48.380
This isn't anything of ours.

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:08.760
She's passionate, smart, attractive, sure I've noticed those things.

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:13.640
She's also unique.

00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.730
Thank you, Hora.

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:23.240
I'll meet him in the transporter room.

00:25:23.630 --> 00:25:24.010
Kirk out.

00:25:25.620 --> 00:25:26.160
Excuse me, doctor.

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:27.350
Jim.

00:25:31.950 --> 00:25:37.940
She's most uncommon. Bones. That's all.

00:26:12.000 --> 00:26:15.430
I've never seen a reading like that before.

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:20.150
Communicator signal? Very faint.

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:21.380
But I can lock on.

00:26:31.440 --> 00:26:34.520
It's a energy discharge.

00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:36.140
Damn it.

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:37.270
Respirator.

00:26:39.380 --> 00:26:41.750
There's no signal to walk on to, sir.

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:43.040
Let me out at Lottie.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:43.640
I know a way.

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:46.210
Fascinating.

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:47.270
I've never seen that dummy.

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:48.050
Don't disturb me.

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:50.130
If her respirator's out, she'll die.

00:26:50.430 --> 00:26:50.990
Try inside.

00:26:52.150 --> 00:26:52.850
I'm going down.

00:26:53.470 --> 00:26:53.710
Sorry.

00:26:55.650 --> 00:26:56.010
Energize.

00:26:56.110 --> 00:26:56.810
Is that a respirator?

00:26:56.910 --> 00:26:57.830
You can't be serious.

00:26:58.130 --> 00:26:59.350
You won't last more than a minute.

00:26:59.640 --> 00:26:59.960
I don't need...

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:01.340
I need a minute. I need 15 seconds.

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:03.660
Put me down at Spock's coordinates. That should be near Carol.

00:27:03.820 --> 00:27:05.180
I will grab her. You beam me back up.

00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:06.700
But, sir... Now, Mr. Scott!

00:27:39.150 --> 00:27:40.910
That was an incredible thing to do.

00:27:41.250 --> 00:27:47.380
To get you to phone something.

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:51.710
From the outpost with an unknown energy reading.

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:52.480
Very good.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:27:56.420
Fine, Spock.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:02.480
Jim, thanks for coming after me.

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:06.770
Need to find out why we lost that transporter lock on you.

00:28:08.690 --> 00:28:09.570
Don't want to lose you again.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:30.840
Beautiful.

00:28:33.132 --> 00:28:34.472
It's like an oasis.

00:28:35.724 --> 00:28:37.724
They say someday every starship will have...

00:28:38.572 --> 00:28:41.572
Holodex, so realistic they won't need an Arboretum.

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:44.500
prefer the real thing.

00:28:45.420 --> 00:28:46.320
So do I.

00:28:46.988 --> 00:28:47.988
Ooh, what are these?

00:28:51.916 --> 00:28:52.236
aroma.

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:55.020
Zanz bericulae.

00:28:55.308 --> 00:28:56.748
They grow everywhere in Lampa 4.

00:28:57.644 --> 00:28:59.284
Yeah, that'll do pretty well on lap of three.

00:29:00.250 --> 00:29:01.590
Once you finished terraforming?

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:03.920
I don't know about that.

00:29:04.060 --> 00:29:04.400
Really?

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:10.300
I came to Lapa III to bring life from lifelessness.

00:29:11.010 --> 00:29:14.570
Instead, I managed to destroy over two dozen human lives.

00:29:15.800 --> 00:29:16.360
The risk?

00:29:16.480 --> 00:29:18.210
Too great.

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:21.040
You're not a quitter.

00:29:21.100 --> 00:29:22.380
You're a scientist.

00:29:23.490 --> 00:29:24.710
Scientist with a conscience.

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:26.380
That's why you can't quit.

00:29:27.060 --> 00:29:28.000
Won't quit.

00:29:28.580 --> 00:29:29.880
You think so?

00:29:31.756 --> 00:29:32.616
Your friends.

00:29:33.004 --> 00:29:34.004
Your colleagues?

00:29:35.244 --> 00:29:37.084
If you quit now, they died in vain.

00:29:38.956 --> 00:29:40.056
You care too much.

00:29:41.228 --> 00:29:41.828
You won't quit.

00:29:45.676 --> 00:29:46.596
You've been there too.

00:29:48.460 --> 00:29:49.180
At what point?

00:29:50.284 --> 00:29:50.844
Tell me, Jim.

00:29:51.948 --> 00:29:53.948
At what point is it too much to carry?

00:29:55.756 --> 00:29:57.256
At what point do you say?

00:29:57.996 --> 00:29:59.196
Enough already.

00:29:59.724 --> 00:29:59.984
I'm just...

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:00.540
Stop.

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:05.640
I'll tell you when I get there.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:08.680
I'm not there yet.

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:12.380
Neither are you.

00:30:15.060 --> 00:30:16.640
Damn you, James T. Kirk.

00:30:19.280 --> 00:30:19.680
You're right.

00:30:21.760 --> 00:30:22.060
That happened.

00:30:53.390 --> 00:30:59.980
effect can isolate fluctuations in the quantum structure of the debris interesting why would you

00:31:00.000 --> 00:31:21.290
do that? A hunch. A hunch? Gotek particles appear to be switching between baryonic and non-baryonic

00:31:21.290 --> 00:31:27.180
states. How is that possible? Our current knowledge of the particles would say it is not.

00:31:31.280 --> 00:31:32.380
I can hardly wait to tell Jim.

00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:34.580
The captain?

00:31:35.110 --> 00:31:39.940
Yes, he would enjoy a discussion on baryonic transitions.

00:31:40.420 --> 00:31:44.810
Still analyzing the debris, Admiral.

00:31:45.220 --> 00:31:45.760
As soon as-

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:47.560
Jim, I'm sorry to put you on the spot.

00:31:47.900 --> 00:31:53.490
Federation Council is raising hell over this, and Starfleet Command is beating down my door for answers.

00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:57.130
We have to know. Is there an alien ship or not?

00:31:57.430 --> 00:31:59.400
Give me three days.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:01.980
We don't have three days, Jim.

00:32:02.660 --> 00:32:05.140
Fitzpatrick wants to assemble Warfleet as we speak.

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:07.660
I'm doing all I can to keep this from escalating.

00:32:08.040 --> 00:32:09.780
But I need you to give me answers, Captain.

00:32:10.280 --> 00:32:12.360
And fast. Starfleet out.

00:32:17.140 --> 00:32:17.420
Enter.

00:32:20.650 --> 00:32:25.620
I, um, have some news.

00:32:27.720 --> 00:32:28.700
Could use some good news.

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:35.030
I don't know if it's good news, it's just that it's the SCOTAC particles.

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:40.170
They're showing up as warbling variables and that shows up as baryonic fluctuations.

00:32:40.550 --> 00:32:42.550
You've been spending too much time with Mr. Spock.

00:32:42.670 --> 00:32:46.940
The point is, I think we've isolated the energy signature.

00:32:47.610 --> 00:32:48.210
Are you hungry?

00:32:48.370 --> 00:32:51.140
No, I just ate nine hours ago.

00:32:52.790 --> 00:32:54.230
Maybe we should have dinner?

00:32:55.140 --> 00:32:57.080
That's a good idea.

00:32:57.640 --> 00:32:58.660
Yes, it is.

00:33:00.380 --> 00:33:02.730
So, um...

00:33:03.420 --> 00:33:03.980
I agree.

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:06.570
Yes, me too.

00:33:06.930 --> 00:33:09.800
I'm not going to dinner, are we?

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:11.520
Not yet.

00:33:30.120 --> 00:33:31.200
Computer, verified data.

00:33:31.720 --> 00:33:32.880
Data verified.

00:33:33.300 --> 00:33:34.020
Encoded disk.

00:33:36.850 --> 00:33:37.510
Spock to bridge.

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:38.350
Bridge here.

00:33:38.670 --> 00:33:39.710
Has the captain arrived yet?

00:33:40.370 --> 00:33:41.090
Not yet, sir.

00:33:43.470 --> 00:33:44.430
Notify me when he does.

00:34:10.840 --> 00:34:11.680
Yes, it is.

00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:24.010
Answer.

00:34:31.820 --> 00:34:32.460
What's the matter?

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:36.910
It's the academy.

00:34:38.310 --> 00:34:39.490
It's not in the books.

00:34:40.420 --> 00:34:41.480
I don't teach you any classes.

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:42.700
Officially.

00:34:44.150 --> 00:34:46.100
Those things you learn from listening.

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:49.400
Share.

00:34:50.420 --> 00:34:50.800
Nice chair.

00:34:51.320 --> 00:34:56.480
You sit in it.

00:35:02.750 --> 00:35:09.880
about that because I was beginning to realize just how right they were.

00:35:18.810 --> 00:35:21.450
Mr. Scott, the subspace probes are now operational.

00:35:23.080 --> 00:35:24.430
Well, it's now or never then.

00:35:26.080 --> 00:35:27.000
Bridge to Kattenkirk.

00:35:29.060 --> 00:35:29.840
We're ready to go, sir.

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:32.250
Initiate course through the dust cloud.

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:35.100
You heard the man.

00:35:35.990 --> 00:35:38.870
Take us to warp 3 until we're within 100,000 kilometers.

00:35:39.470 --> 00:35:41.530
And then half impulse as we approach the cloud.

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:42.540
Yes, sir.

00:35:42.780 --> 00:35:58.890
We should arrive at the dust cloud.

00:36:00.190 --> 00:36:01.290
37 minutes, sir.

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:04.510
Passive sensors standing by.

00:36:05.210 --> 00:36:05.730
Let's do it.

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:09.280
Let's cover this search pattern quickly and quietly,

00:36:10.020 --> 00:36:11.100
on tiptoes if necessary.

00:36:12.380 --> 00:36:13.880
Like a leaf on the solar wind.

00:36:21.290 --> 00:36:22.050
I have to get to the bridge.

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:23.770
I have a job too.

00:36:24.760 --> 00:36:25.300
Yes, you do.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:32.100
Are you going to get that?

00:36:34.170 --> 00:36:34.490
Yes.

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:39.050
Come in.

00:36:39.190 --> 00:36:44.420
Did not mean to intrude.

00:36:45.230 --> 00:36:45.570
It's all right.

00:36:46.300 --> 00:36:47.300
I was just headed to the bridge.

00:36:48.470 --> 00:36:49.010
It's on your mind.

00:36:50.230 --> 00:36:52.730
I believe I know what caused the explosion on lap of three.

00:37:00.320 --> 00:37:03.800
Captain, with all due respect to Dr. Marcus, you may want to hear this along.

00:37:04.960 --> 00:37:08.470
He has a vested interest in this too, Spock. Let's hear it.

00:37:11.460 --> 00:37:11.800
Very well.

00:37:14.250 --> 00:37:17.610
As we suspected, the explosion did not occur in the outpost reactors.

00:37:19.220 --> 00:37:23.820
It came from an unintended standing wave from the terraforming system's main pattern, matrices.

00:37:25.460 --> 00:37:28.520
There was an interaction and an unpredictable phenomenon.

00:37:29.100 --> 00:37:29.640
I don't understand.

00:37:30.190 --> 00:37:33.020
The pattern matrices focus the arrangement of molecules.

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:34.500
There's no way it can cause an explosion.

00:37:34.820 --> 00:37:35.520
Correct, Doctor.

00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:38.300
Except the debris we recovered from the planet's surface

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:41.690
was contaminated with protomatter, sir.

00:37:44.700 --> 00:37:45.180
Protomatter?

00:37:46.020 --> 00:37:49.600
Protomatter is a theoretical vestige of the birth of the universe.

00:37:50.850 --> 00:37:51.930
It's highly unstable.

00:37:52.150 --> 00:37:55.780
It fluctuates at the quantum level between matter and antimatter states.

00:37:57.060 --> 00:37:59.520
And if they came into existence at the same moment...

00:38:01.120 --> 00:38:07.620
cause an explosion, big enough to destroy the outpost and a huge chunk of the planet's surface.

00:38:07.840 --> 00:38:11.970
No. No, no, no. It isn't possible. We couldn't have-

00:38:11.970 --> 00:38:16.110
It was always a possibility, Doctor. A possibility that you yourself acknowledged

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:18.410
in the theoretical basis of the entire project.

00:38:18.590 --> 00:38:22.770
A possibility so beyond the realm of probability, it was statistically insignificant.

00:38:23.010 --> 00:38:27.810
Nonetheless, the experiment created protomatter, and the protomatter was unstable.

00:38:27.970 --> 00:38:29.030
I want to review your data.

00:38:29.470 --> 00:38:29.750
Of course.

00:38:30.670 --> 00:38:31.830
So you will find no errors?

00:38:31.990 --> 00:38:32.490
Of course not.

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:34.420
Vulcans are incapable of being wrong.

00:38:34.660 --> 00:38:37.840
No, Vulcans are unwilling to be inaccurate.

00:38:40.780 --> 00:38:41.980
Important question now, sir,

00:38:42.220 --> 00:38:44.960
is whether or not the protomatter was created intentionally.

00:38:45.200 --> 00:38:47.660
It was not an accident.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:49.540
And it was sabotage.

00:38:49.820 --> 00:38:50.520
Okay, that's enough.

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:55.070
Mr. Spock, I want you to let Dr. Marcus review your work.

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:57.670
I assure you my work is most thorough.

00:38:58.080 --> 00:38:59.640
That is not why she needs to see it.

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:02.220
Do you understand?

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:15.900
Thank you.

00:39:16.390 --> 00:39:16.670
Think.

00:39:18.820 --> 00:39:19.140
You're welcome.

00:39:22.970 --> 00:39:23.110
Shh.

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:46.230
We're taking a second pass on the search grid now.

00:39:47.660 --> 00:39:47.960
Anything?

00:39:48.580 --> 00:39:49.280
Negative, Captain.

00:39:49.600 --> 00:39:52.880
The particulates in the cloud make passive sensors new and effective.

00:39:54.490 --> 00:39:54.890
Options.

00:39:56.070 --> 00:39:58.530
Can use active probes or active sensors.

00:39:59.360 --> 00:39:59.980
Can't say, like,

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:06.970
either of those choices. There is a third possibility. Mr. Sulu, we have enough visual

00:40:06.970 --> 00:40:12.260
data to assemble a holographic schematic of the dust cloud, yes? Yes, sir. When a ship

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:18.160
travels under impulse power, it leaves a faint disturbance awake. Let's look for a trail.

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:19.780
Here we go.

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:32.220
Mr. Sulu, intercept.

00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:59.920
Oximity alert. Confirmed. Object dead ahead. Approximately 200 meters in length.

00:41:00.150 --> 00:41:01.950
Distance 112 kilometers.

00:41:03.220 --> 00:41:04.870
Captain, dust is clearing.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:18.980
Frequencies.

00:41:19.250 --> 00:41:20.390
Alien frequencies open.

00:41:22.040 --> 00:41:25.080
Alien vessels, this is Captain James T. Kirk,

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:27.640
the Federation Starship Enterprise.

00:41:27.780 --> 00:41:29.440
We are not hostile.

00:41:30.190 --> 00:41:31.410
Please identify yourself.

00:41:32.000 --> 00:41:33.120
She's charging her weapons.

00:41:33.400 --> 00:41:34.100
All back full.

00:41:34.490 --> 00:41:35.530
All hands red alert.

00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:36.530
Raise shields.

00:41:36.730 --> 00:41:37.730
Stand by phasers.

00:42:08.320 --> 00:42:10.660
Photon torpedoes. No damage.

00:42:11.790 --> 00:42:13.470
They can wing those at us all day long.

00:42:14.510 --> 00:42:15.290
Scratch our shields.

00:42:15.860 --> 00:42:18.120
So, not outgunned.

00:42:18.220 --> 00:42:21.140
Let's try again. Open hailing frequencies.

00:42:21.710 --> 00:42:22.910
Hailing frequencies open.

00:42:25.080 --> 00:42:27.760
Alien vessel. This is Captain James T. Kirk.

00:42:28.100 --> 00:42:29.740
The Federation Starship Enterprise.

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:33.550
We are not hostile. We are on a research mission.

00:42:34.550 --> 00:42:36.070
Please, identify yourself.

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:39.520
No response, sir.

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:43.590
Bring us into firing position and give me one short phaser burst.

00:42:44.100 --> 00:42:46.550
Don't hurt them. Just shake them up.

00:42:54.880 --> 00:42:57.320
I'm out of image to the alien ship's forward shields.

00:42:58.430 --> 00:42:59.770
They have stopped pursuing us.

00:43:00.670 --> 00:43:01.890
Secure if I'm all back full.

00:43:02.580 --> 00:43:02.960
Aye, sir.

00:43:03.340 --> 00:43:04.440
Engines at station keeping.

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:06.460
Now let's see if they're ready to talk.

00:43:07.180 --> 00:43:07.980
Open frequencies.

00:43:09.550 --> 00:43:10.550
Channel open, sir.

00:43:12.010 --> 00:43:12.650
Alien vessel.

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:16.790
This is Captain James T. Kirk, the Starship Enterprise.

00:43:18.020 --> 00:43:20.240
We represent the United Federation of Planets.

00:43:21.670 --> 00:43:22.490
Identify yourself.

00:43:23.900 --> 00:43:25.980
We owe you no explanations.

00:43:26.900 --> 00:43:28.900
We have the right to defend ourselves.

00:43:31.400 --> 00:43:37.140
Search mission. Identify yourself.

00:43:37.340 --> 00:43:42.820
Your federation has been working to transform the environment of the third planet.

00:43:43.480 --> 00:43:46.280
That is a hostile act.

00:43:47.450 --> 00:43:50.030
We were not aware that anyone laid claim to this world.

00:43:51.290 --> 00:43:52.230
You didn't ask.

00:43:54.300 --> 00:43:55.320
They do have a point.

00:43:59.440 --> 00:43:59.840
Very helpful.

00:44:01.180 --> 00:44:05.330
Our mission is to explore, to seek out new life and new civilizations.

00:44:05.510 --> 00:44:08.070
There is much we can learn from each other.

00:44:09.640 --> 00:44:11.400
Can we be partners in discovery?

00:44:11.850 --> 00:44:13.530
Where is a prophet in that?

00:44:14.770 --> 00:44:15.190
Excuse me?

00:44:16.690 --> 00:44:19.260
We're not interested in discovery.

00:44:20.060 --> 00:44:21.700
We're not interested in friends.

00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:25.030
We have the best friend's money to buy.

00:44:26.380 --> 00:44:28.320
We're interested in commerce.

00:44:32.380 --> 00:44:46.710
I propose we negotiate a trade zone.

00:44:48.650 --> 00:44:54.190
Federation is interested in profit as well.

00:44:54.370 --> 00:44:59.980
Captain Kirk, people are well aware of our commercial income.

00:45:00.000 --> 00:45:03.530
We've already conducted trade with them.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:07.300
We aren't aware of any trade with our peoples.

00:45:09.090 --> 00:45:19.310
Then I should advise you that the only thing more expensive than a question is the answer.

00:45:23.580 --> 00:45:24.080
But...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.800
I'm curious. They have very strange ears.

00:45:39.030 --> 00:45:46.120
Matrux, is there any record of Federation contact with some alien species out here?

00:45:46.880 --> 00:45:53.240
None that I'm aware of, but I can see no tactical advantage in claiming trade agreement where none exists.

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:57.700
There's only one group they could have done business with. Team on lap of three.

00:45:58.850 --> 00:45:59.110
Jim.

00:46:00.060 --> 00:46:05.160
If that is the case, it is highly unlikely that Dr. Marcus knew nothing about it.

00:46:05.420 --> 00:46:06.320
That's not logical.

00:46:08.550 --> 00:46:10.960
No, that is not Dr. Marcus.

00:46:11.220 --> 00:46:12.960
I assume you have evidence.

00:46:22.480 --> 00:46:22.920
Ferengi?

00:46:24.390 --> 00:46:24.830
Ferengi.

00:46:24.890 --> 00:46:26.330
